# Migration files are checksummed byte-for-byte, so their line endings are part
# of the build.
#
# `lib.rs` embeds each migration with `include_str!`, which copies the working
# tree's bytes verbatim, and sqlx hashes that text to decide whether a migration
# has changed since it was applied. With `core.autocrlf=true` -- the Windows
# default, and what this repo was being built under -- git checks these files out
# as CRLF while storing them as LF. The result is that the same commit produces a
# different checksum on a Windows checkout than on CI or on a colleague's machine
# with `autocrlf=false`, and a user who upgrades across two such builds is met
# with "migration 1 was previously applied but has been modified" about a file
# nobody has touched since the first commit.
#
# Pinning them to LF makes the embedded bytes a property of the commit rather
# than of whoever checked it out. LF is the right side to pin to: it is what git
# already stores, what a Linux or CI build produces, and what the databases in
# the field were created with.
#
# `scripts/check-migration-eol.mjs` enforces this, because a .gitattributes file
# only governs a fresh checkout -- a file already present with CRLF stays CRLF.
*.sql text eol=lf
